WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.159 --> 00:00:08.010
Do you write cursively with beautifully flowing,
looped letters or do you print?

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:13.420
Actually, a more pressing question would be:
Do you write at all?

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:17.300
Can you remember the last time you put pen
to paper, instead of finger to keyboard?

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.039
Don’t worry, you’re not alone.

00:00:20.039 --> 00:00:26.970
The statistics show that every year more and
more people are rapidly forgoing penmanship

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:32.599
altogether in favour of typing, even for small
written tasks such as shopping lists or reminders

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:36.399
on sticky notes, most of us have replaced
these with apps.

00:00:36.399 --> 00:00:42.070
A recent British survey found that one-third
of adults hadn't written anything by hand

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:44.079
in the past six months.

00:00:44.079 --> 00:00:50.390
I too am dangerously close to counting myself
amongst their number, but I think that’s

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.239
a great shame.

00:00:52.239 --> 00:01:01.360
Furthermore, this trend is damaging to our
intelligence as a species.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:08.140
The humble pen, the great bastion of written
communication for over five-thousand years.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:16.220
The Sumerians created the first written communication
in 3200 BC in Mesopotamia when they carved

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:19.660
out cuneiform script into stone.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:27.140
The foundation of Western freedoms Magna Carta
was inked into sheepskin with beautiful Latin

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.840
cursive at Runneymeade in 1215.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:36.750
Then, in 1776 the Declaration of Independence
was signed, written in a 17th-century script

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.560
called English Roundhand.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:45.489
Which was mostly superseded in the late nineteenth
century by Spencerian script - you have seen

00:01:45.489 --> 00:01:52.110
Spencerian script if you have ever seen, what
one could argue is a rather popular logo…

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:54.890
this (coca cola).

00:01:54.890 --> 00:02:00.000
Handwriting is a deep part of our culture
but today there is a concerted yet contentious

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.350
effort by schools worldwide to move children
away from the pen and in front of computer

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:10.289
screens or in front of tablets, tapping away
like lemmings.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:15.570
The logic being that these devices are what
they will be using in the workplace anyway,

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:20.720
so why get them accustomed to an antiquated
writing system that may be of little to no

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:22.880
use in their career?

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:24.270
I’ll tell you why.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:28.810
Have you ever been amongst a large crowd of
people talking at once, perhaps in a busy

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:30.590
restaurant or at a dinner party?

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:34.410
Maybe you are thoroughly engaged in a conversation
with somebody yourself.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:40.320
When, all of a sudden, from the noisy static
of the crowd your brain picks out a single

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:47.220
word or sentence and you hear it as clear
as day, even from the other side of the room.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:51.230
It could be your name or a bit of gossip about
somebody you know.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:57.190
What just happened was your brain’s Reticular
Activating System (RAS) activated.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:03.010
At all times there are literally millions
of bits of data in your surroundings, noises,

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:06.480
smells, sights, and physical sensations.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.990
This is far too much for your brain to comprehend.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:14.760
Multiple tests over the years have shown that
the brain is only capable of focusing on a

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:19.320
maximum of four different things at once,
and even that is optimistic.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:23.650
Most of the time we focus on one to two things
simultaneously.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:28.740
So how does our brain decide from which of
the potentially millions of possible things

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:33.880
to focus on are worth our current time and
attention?

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.660
It employs a conductor.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:42.420
Sitting at the central base of the brain,
the brain’s doorway to sensory inputs, it

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.700
acts as a filter.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.730
All the information around us is constantly
knocking on the door of our brain for further

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:54.830
processing, it is the job of the RAS to decide
what to let in.

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:59.280
When we put pen to paper the RAS is activated.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:07.060
Because writing requires fine motor control
and the majority of our focus our RAS can’t

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:12.700
help but prioritise whatever we are currently
writing about as the most important processing

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:15.290
job for our brain.

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:20.970
Conversely, when text is typed every keystroke
is exactly the same, there is no difference

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:26.960
in motor control between pressing the G key
to the A key.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:33.811
Once proficient our brains can type almost
autonomously, without much thought, and so

00:04:33.811 --> 00:04:40.620
the RAS can filter out much of the information
we are taking in whilst typing, a luxury it

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.670
doesn’t have whilst writing.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:47.510
A 2010 study confirmed this with children.

00:04:47.510 --> 00:04:51.910
When they were asked to write words such as
‘spaceship’ by hand the areas of the brain

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:54.360
associated with learning lit up.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:05:01.220
When they typed the same words, their brain
activity was a lot quieter, as though someone

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:03.530
had turned off the lights.

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:08.530
Put yourself in the hypothetical scenario
of viewing a University lecture and you must

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:12.300
take notes so you can learn vital knowledge
for an upcoming exam.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:17.990
You have a choice of either using a laptop
or a simple notepad and pen to record your

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:21.870
notes - which would you choose?

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:27.850
If you chose the laptop then you are likely
to do much worse on the exam.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:34.240
As researchers, Pam Mueller from Princeton
and Daniel Oppenheimer from California University

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.930
found out in a 2014 study.

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:42.389
A group of students were asked to watch five
TED talks in a lecture hall and take notes

00:05:42.389 --> 00:05:43.389
on them.

00:05:43.389 --> 00:05:48.370
Half of the students were given laptops with
no internet connection, to prevent online

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:53.199
distractions and the other half were given
only a pen and paper.

00:05:53.199 --> 00:05:57.150
After a 30 minute break, the participants
were asked a series of questions that required

00:05:57.150 --> 00:06:00.990
knowledge from the TED talks to answer.

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:07.050
The students who took longhand notes performed
significantly better at answering the questions

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:10.040
than the laptop note takers.

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:15.820
Mueller and Oppenheimer think this is because
when we type notes we usually copy whatever

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:20.940
the lecturer is saying verbatim without attempting
to summarise it.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:26.720
However, because handwriting is too slow to
write every single word being spoken we are

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:30.550
forced to summarise the lecture’s main points.

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:37.410
In doing so the brain spends much longer processing
the information it is taking in, Mueller and

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:41.050
Oppenheimer refer to this process as ‘encoding’.

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:46.830
Conversely, when we type the lecture notes
word-for-word we are using the laptop as external

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:48.979
storage for our brain.

00:06:48.979 --> 00:06:55.830
Sure, we are hearing and recording every word
with keystrokes, but because this semi-automatic

00:06:55.830 --> 00:07:04.330
process requires little thought or contemplation
our brain is not actually processing (encoding)

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:10.639
the information, it is merely entering our
ears and passing straight through our brain

00:07:10.639 --> 00:07:13.210
into our fingers.

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:18.009
When notes are handwritten, because we must
think about the meaning of what is being said

00:07:18.009 --> 00:07:23.840
and summarise it in a very short amount of
time, a significantly higher amount of neural

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:28.140
processing or ‘encoding’ is required.

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:33.669
And so, even though we are taking far fewer
notes, overall we remember the notes we have

00:07:33.669 --> 00:07:37.630
written far, far better, than when typed.

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:43.240
Furthermore, you are more likely to understand
the meaning behind those notes afterwards,

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:48.180
instead of staring blankly at the five-thousand
word document on your computer screen and

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:52.790
wanting to hammer your head into the keyboard
as you suddenly realise that you have absolutely

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:55.449
no idea what any of it means.

00:07:55.449 --> 00:08:00.550
Interestingly, they repeated the study once
more, but this time allowed both groups to

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:05.139
re-read and study their notes for some time
after the lectures.

00:08:05.139 --> 00:08:12.300
It would make sense that if the laptop notetakers
could revise their word-for-word notes they

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:18.860
would then be able to remember more - but
astonishingly this made little difference

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:24.870
on their learning - the longhand notetakers
still did much better.

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:30.970
It seems that the encoding process the longhand
notetakers did when first hearing the information

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:35.930
was invaluable for their retention and understanding.

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:44.310
But to me, there is one overwhelming reason
we should fight to keep handwriting alive.

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:49.399
Because it can be absolutely beautiful.

00:08:49.399 --> 00:08:54.269
Calligraphy is an art form and all those who
practice it desire to reach such a level of

00:08:54.269 --> 00:09:01.439
mastery that they are invited to an exclusive
society with a really cool and memorable name,

00:09:01.439 --> 00:09:08.870
the ‘International Association of Master
Penmen, Engrossers and Teachers of Handwriting’

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:13.480
- it may not have a pretty name, but my god
does it have a pretty logo.

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:19.050
IAMPETH, as it is commonly shortened to are
an international organisation responsible

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:21.839
for choosing master penmen.

00:09:21.839 --> 00:09:26.999
A master penman is a calligrapher who has
reached a distinguished level of mastery in

00:09:26.999 --> 00:09:28.490
calligraphic arts.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:35.209
There are many stages to qualify as a master
penman, but the final is they must rather

00:09:35.209 --> 00:09:38.189
aptly, create their own certificate.

00:09:38.189 --> 00:09:45.319
There are currently only twelve master penmen
in the world and they create beautiful works

00:09:45.319 --> 00:09:57.519
of art like these.

00:09:57.519 --> 00:10:02.730
Technology is amazing and if we all abandoned
it for the pen then the modern world would

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:04.519
grind to a halt.

00:10:04.519 --> 00:10:10.100
But I think it’s really important that the
art form of writing by hand is kept alive

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:13.989
as we rush ceaselessly into a technological
future.

00:10:13.989 --> 00:10:18.959
IAMPETH is certainly grasping to keep the
art of penmanship and all its history and

00:10:18.959 --> 00:10:24.509
culture alive, but twelve master penman can
only do so much.

00:10:24.509 --> 00:10:28.790
If we all try to take a moment every now and
then to simply pen a letter to a friend or

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:33.899
if you’re feeling more adventurous, try
your hand at calligraphy then, collectively,

00:10:33.899 --> 00:10:39.459
we can preserve the very thing that built
our world.

